<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tajikistan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Tajikistan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tajikistan, officially the Republic of Tajikistan, is a landlocked country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Parts of its territory belonged to the Sogdian and Bactrian civilisations and were ruled in turn by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanid Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanate-of-bukhara">Khanate of Bukhara</a>, before conquest by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. Its borders were drawn while it was part of Uzbekistan as the Tajik ASSR, and it became a Soviet republic on 5 December 1929. Tajikistan declared independence on 9 September 1991. A civil war followed from May 1992 to June 1997, with estimated deaths above 100,000.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tajikistan.jpg" alt="Tajikistan" width="330" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tajikistan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tajikistan is landlocked and is the smallest country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> by area. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> to the south, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> to the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a> to the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to the east, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wakhan">Wakhan Corridor</a> of Afghanistan separates it narrowly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. Mountains of the Pamir range cover more than 90 percent of the country, and most of it lies more than 3,000 metres above sea level. The lower land is in the north, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Fergana Valley</a>, and in the southern valleys of the Kofarnihon and Vakhsh rivers, which form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a>. The Amu Darya and the Panj mark the border with Afghanistan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Cultures in the region date back to at least the fourth millennium BCE, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>–Margiana Archaeological Complex, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronovo-culture">Andronovo culture</a> and the pre-urban site of Sarazm, now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanid Empire</a> (819–999) began with four brothers, each ruling territory under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> suzerainty. In 892 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-ibn-ahmad">Ismail Samani</a> united the state under a single ruler, and it became independent. The Samanids developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> as cultural centres of the Iranian world. The area came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanate-of-bukhara">Khanate of Bukhara</a> in the 16th century.</p><p>Between 1864 and 1885 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> took control of all of Russian Turkestan, including the parts of present-day Tajikistan held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a>. In February 1990 riots and strikes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dushanbe">Dushanbe</a> over housing and unemployment drew in the nationalist and democratic opposition and Islamists, and Soviet Internal Troops were brought in. After independence the country fell into civil war between factions marked by clan and regional loyalties. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gharm">Gharm</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorno-badakhshan-autonomous-region">Gorno-Badakhshan</a> groups, led by liberal reformers and Islamists who became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-tajik-opposition">United Tajik Opposition</a>, rose against the government of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rahmon-nabiyev">Rahmon Nabiyev</a>, in which people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khujand">Khujand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulob">Kulob</a> predominated. Foreign states supported different factions. All but 25,000 of more than 400,000 ethnic Russians left, and a peace agreement ended the war by 1997.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dushanbe">Dushanbe</a> is the capital and largest city, with over 1.2 million people. Tajikistan is a presidential republic of four administrative divisions: the provinces of Sughd and Khatlon, the autonomous province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorno-badakhshan-autonomous-region">Gorno-Badakhshan</a> and the Regions of Republican Subordination. Tajik is the state language and Russian the language of inter-ethnic communication. The Yaghnobi language, spoken by about 25,000 people in the north, is the only direct descendant of Sogdian. Sarazm is on the World Heritage List, and the country is a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> and the CIS and the OSCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tajikistan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Takht-i Sangin in <b>Tajikistan</b> held a large temple to the Oxus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Mirziyoyev relations with Kyrgyzstan, <b>Tajikistan</b> and Afghanistan improved drastically, and ties with China, Turkey, Russia, India, South Korea, Japan, the UAE and the West have grown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Middle Persian is the linguistic ancestor of New Persian, the official language of Iran, of Afghanistan as Dari and of <b>Tajikistan</b> as Tajik.</blockquote><a href="/language/middle-persian">Middle Persian · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>Tajikistan</b>, Persian has been written in the Cyrillic alphabet since 1940.</blockquote><a href="/language/persian">Persian · Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</a></li><li><blockquote>In the highlands of <b>Tajikistan</b>, kurgan cemeteries of the Vaksh and Bishkent types mixed late BMAC and Tazabagyab-Andronovo traditions, and at Sappali Tepe metal objects of 1700–1500 BCE derive from the Tazabagyab-Andronovo culture.</blockquote><a href="/culture/oxus-civilisation">Oxus civilisation · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sogdians were an Iranian people whose homeland, Sogdiana, lay between the Amu Darya and the Syr Darya, the Oxus and Jaxartes of the Greeks, in present-day Uzbekistan and <b>Tajikistan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sogdians">Sogdians · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
